@@ -0,0 +1,2329 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * QuickLogic CSSP camera interface driver
+ *
+ * The CSSP ("CAM I/F") is a bridge between an 8-bit parallel camera sensor
+ * and the 16-bit address/data multiplexed TI GPMC bus. It is used on the
+ * BeagleBone camera capes. The bridge has a single mode register and a pixel
+ * data port, no interrupt and no frame size or format registers. It raises a
+ * DMA request each time 32 or 128 bytes of pixel data are ready (depending
+ * on the silicon revision), and the frame size is purely the DMA length.
+ *
+ * No datasheet exists. The register usage and all timings follow the 3.8
+ * driver written for the capes.
+ *
+ * DMA design
+ * ----------
+ *
+ * The data is moved by one cyclic dmaengine transfer into a ring of N
+ * frame-sized bounce slots (one period per frame). The DMA controller links
+ * the periods into a hardware loop, so there is no CPU step and no lost DMA
+ * request between frames. A work item copies each completed slot into the
+ * next queued vb2 buffer; when no buffer is queued the frame is dropped and
+ * the sequence number still advances.
+ *
+ * The period callbacks coalesce, so frame progress is derived from the DMA
+ * position, sampled and applied under qlock (cssp_account()). Only frames
+ * that a period callback reported complete are copied; a position sample
+ * taken by the copy work only decides whether a slot was overwritten.
+ *
+ * The bridge has no frame markers. Alignment rests on arming the DMA before
+ * the capture is enabled, on periods that are exactly one frame, and on the
+ * assumption that the bridge starts capturing at a frame boundary. A period
+ * that completes in the middle of a frame (a non-zero offset into the next
+ * period when the callback runs) is the only observable sign of a lost DMA
+ * request or a FIFO overflow. Frames around such a callback are completed
+ * with an error, and repeated offsets trigger a recovery that restarts the
+ * DMA and pulses the capture enable bit.
+ *
+ * Frame sizes
+ * -----------
+ *
+ * Each DMA request moves a fixed number of bytes (128 on revision > 3, 32
+ * otherwise), so sizeimage must be a multiple of the request length. The
+ * format ioctls accept any size within the limits because libcamera needs
+ * S_FMT to return the requested size and the constraint is a product of
+ * width and height. A size that does not satisfy it fails at STREAMON with
+ * -EPIPE. For 16-bit formats on a revision > 3 bridge, width x height must
+ * be a multiple of 64; for 8-bit raw formats a multiple of 128.
+ *
+ * Locking
+ * -------
+ *
+ * Lock order: vlock -> bridge subdev state lock -> sensor locks -> hw_lock
+ * -> qlock (-> the DMA driver's channel lock, only inside
+ * dmaengine_tx_status()). Work items never take vlock or a subdev lock.
+ */
+
